Navi48Bringup — Phase 3 GPU bring-up kext for Navi 48 (gfx1201) on x86 macOS.

This work reuses and adapts code from two upstream projects, with thanks:

* RDNA4FB  (BSD 3-Clause, (c) 2026 Sunneva N. Mariu / somestupidgirl)
  https://github.com/somestupidgirl/RDNA4FB
  - src/ipdiscovery.{hpp,cpp} are used verbatim (their BSD-3 headers retained).
  - The BAR5 register-mapping, indirect-VRAM (MM_INDEX/MM_DATA), on-die IP
    discovery, and read-only PSP/SMU survey techniques are ported from it.

* mac-amdgpu  (MIT, lemonade-sdk)
  https://github.com/lemonade-sdk/mac-amdgpu
  - The IP-block bring-up sequences (gfx/gmc/psp/smu/sdma/mes/ih v12/v14/v7)
    that later writing stages will port are derived from this project, which
    proved the full Navi 48 bring-up on macOS (Apple Silicon / DriverKit).

Register semantics are cross-checked against the Linux amdgpu driver. The AMD
GPU code in Linux (drivers/gpu/drm/amd: amdgpu, display/dc, dmub, pm, include)
is MIT-licensed by AMD, apart from a few files this project does not use
(amdgpu_vkms, amdgpu_atpx_handler); checked file by file at Linux 7.2 (8d3ae59).
The tools build against commit 238650ef6c7c (the 7.3 merge window), the one
tools/dcn41 cites. The licence text is in third-party/THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.txt.
* src/dcn41/dcn41_regs.h and dcn41_allow_ranges.h are generated from
  include/asic_reg/dcn/dcn_4_1_0_offset.h and dcn_4_1_0_sh_mask.h. Unlike the
  rest of include/asic_reg, those two headers carry no licence text (the offset
  header says only "Copyright 2024 Advanced Micro Devices, Inc."); what is used
  from them is register offsets, masks and shifts.
* tools/dcn41/harness.py, tools/dcn41/dml and tools/dcn41/dptrain compile
  unmodified Linux DC sources on the build host (register programming, DML2.1,
  DP link training) to compare against src/dcn41; they are not part of the kext.
* src/dcn41/dcn41_dp_train.c is a port of dc/link's 8b/10b link training.
